This <arithmetic series> has $101$ terms, from $1$ to $201$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{100}(2n+1)=\frac{101(1+201)}2=101^2=10201.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <geometric series> has first term $1$ and common ratio $1/3$, whose <absolute value> is less than one. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\left(\frac13\right)^n=\frac1{1-1/3}=\frac32.}
$$

= 6
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

On this <parametric curve>, $x=1$ permits $t=0$ or $t=-2$, but only $t=0$ also gives $y=0$. The <chain rule> gives the <slope>
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}=\frac{12-3t^2}{2(1+t)}.
$$
At the required point, $t=0$, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=6.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The double-angle identity for the <sine> factors the equation as $2\cos\theta(\sin\theta-\cos\theta)=0$. The first factor gives $\theta=\pi/2$ in the specified interval; the second gives $\tan\theta=1$ and hence $\theta=\pi/4$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\theta=\frac\pi4,\quad\frac\pi2.}
$$
Keeping the first factor avoids losing the solution where division by $\cos\theta$ would be invalid.

= 8
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Call the centre of the <circle> $O$, the left endpoint on the diameter $A$, the point of tangency $T$, and the external intersection $E$. Since $OA=OT$ are <radii>, the <isosceles triangle> $AOT$ has equal base <angles>: $\angle OAT=\angle ATO=30^\circ$. Therefore $\angle AOT=120^\circ$, and, because $OA$ and $OE$ point in opposite directions, $\angle TOE=60^\circ$. The <tangent to a circle> $TE$ is perpendicular to the <radius> $OT$, so in the right <triangle> $OTE$,
$$
\boxed{\theta=180^\circ-90^\circ-60^\circ=30^\circ.}
$$

= 9
{parent=Section A}
