= Solution
{parent=a}

The <function> is nonnegative and satisfies $\cos^2(\pi-x)=\cos^2x$. Its <derivative> is $-\sin(2x)$, so it decreases from $(0,1)$ to its <minimum> $(\pi/2,0)$ and then increases to $(\pi,1)$. These three points all have horizontal <tangent lines>.

\Image[paper-1-elementary-graphs.png]
{title=The cosine-squared curve and the two branches of the exponential quotient}
{description=The left panel shows the symmetric minimum of $\cos^2x$. The right panel marks the vertical and horizontal <asymptotes> of $1/(1-e^x)$.}

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The denominator vanishes only at $x=0$, giving a vertical <asymptote>. Since $1-e^x=-x+O(x^2)$, the <function> tends to $+\infty$ from the left and $-\infty$ from the right. Its <derivative> is
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\frac1{1-e^x}=\frac{e^x}{(1-e^x)^2}>0,
$$
so each branch is increasing. The horizontal <asymptotes> are $y=1$ as $x\to-\infty$ and $y=0$ as $x\to+\infty$. The left branch lies above $1$; the right branch lies below $0$, as in the preceding figure.

