= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because $\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf x$ is a <scalar>, applying the <linear operator> again gives
$$
K^2\mathbf x=K\bigl[\mathbf a(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf x)\bigr]=\mathbf a\bigl[\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf x)\bigr]=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)K\mathbf x.
$$
This is the first nontrivial case of the <powers of a rank-one operator>; its <matrix> is the <outer product> $\mathbf a\mathbf b^T$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <vector triple product identity> and $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf n=1$ give
$$
H^2\mathbf x=\alpha^2\mathbf n\times(\mathbf n\times\mathbf x)=\alpha^2\bigl[\mathbf n(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf x)-\mathbf x\bigr].
$$
Apply $H$ once more. The term parallel to $\mathbf n$ vanishes under its <cross product> with $\mathbf n$, leaving $H^3\mathbf x=-\alpha^3\mathbf n\times\mathbf x=-\alpha^2H\mathbf x$. The result includes $\alpha=0$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The displayed <power series> is the <matrix exponential> $G=e^H$. From $H^3=-\alpha^2H$, its odd powers and nonconstant even powers sum separately:
$$
G=I+\left(1-\frac{\alpha^2}{3!}+\frac{\alpha^4}{5!}-\cdots\right)H+\left(\frac1{2!}-\frac{\alpha^2}{4!}+\frac{\alpha^4}{6!}-\cdots\right)H^2.
$$
The scalar <power series> are $\sin\alpha/\alpha$ and $(1-\cos\alpha)/\alpha^2$, with continuous values $1$ and $1/2$ at zero. Substituting $H\mathbf x=\alpha\mathbf n\times\mathbf x$ and the preceding formula for $H^2\mathbf x$ yields
$$
G\mathbf x=\mathbf x+\sin\alpha\,\mathbf n\times\mathbf x+(1-\cos\alpha)\mathbf n\times(\mathbf n\times\mathbf x).
$$
This is the <Rodrigues rotation formula>: the component parallel to $\mathbf n$ is fixed, while the perpendicular component undergoes a <rotation> through $\alpha$. For $\alpha=0$ the <linear operator> is the <identity operator>.

= 2
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Using the <principal complex logarithm>, $\operatorname{Log}i=i\pi/2$ and $\operatorname{Log}(-e)=1+i\pi$. Thus the values and their <real parts> and <imaginary parts> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{expression}&\text{principal value}&\operatorname{Re}&\operatorname{Im}\\\hline
\sqrt i&(1+i)/\sqrt2&1/\sqrt2&1/\sqrt2\\
\ln(-e)&1+i\pi&1&\pi\\
2^i&e^{i\ln2}&\cos(\ln2)&\sin(\ln2)\\
(i^i)^i&-i&0&-1
\end{array}
$$
For the final row, first calculate $i^i=e^{-\pi/2}$ and only then raise this positive real number to the power $i$. Treating nested powers as ordinary real powers would ignore the branching of <complex exponentiation>.

If all branches are requested, $\sqrt i$ also has the negative of its principal value, and the <complex logarithm> of $-e$ is $1+i(\pi+2\pi k)$. Allowing nonprincipal logarithms of the positive base $2$ gives $e^{-2\pi k}e^{i\ln2}$. Finally, all values of $(i^i)^i$ are $-ie^{-2\pi m}$, for integers $k,m$ as appropriate. These alternatives do not alter the principal-value table.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Euler's formula> gives
$$
\sin x=\frac{e^{ix}-e^{-ix}}{2i},\qquad\cos x=\frac{e^{ix}+e^{-ix}}2.
$$
Cubing the expression for the <sine>, and grouping the $e^{3ix}$ and $e^{-3ix}$ terms, gives
$$
\sin^3x=-\frac14\bigl(\sin3x-3\sin x\bigr),\qquad\sin3x=3\sin x-4\sin^3x.
$$
The required equation therefore becomes $\sin x(1-4\sin^2x)=0$. The complete real solution set is
$$
\boxed{x=n\pi\quad\text{or}\quad x=n\pi\pm\frac\pi6,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <quadratic formula> gives $z=-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-1}$. For $-1\leq b\leq1$, write this as
$$
\boxed{z_\pm=-b\pm i\sqrt{1-b^2}.}
$$
Each <root of a polynomial> has <modulus> $1$, since $b^2+(1-b^2)=1$. As $b$ increases from $-1$ to $1$, the upper root traverses the upper unit semicircle from $1$ to $-1$, and the lower root traverses the lower semicircle in the opposite angular direction. At $b=-1$ the roots coincide at $1$; at $b=1$ they coincide at $-1$. For intermediate $b$ they are <complex conjugates>.

\Image[paper-1-quadratic-root-locus.png]
{title=The conjugate roots travel along the two unit semicircles}
{description=Arrows show increasing $b$, with endpoints $b=-1$ and $b=1$ and the midpoint pair $b=0$. The figure is an original plot of the solution formula.}

