= Solution
{parent=i}

Since the <derivative> of the <arctangent> is $1/(1+x^2)=1-x^2+O(x^4)$ near zero, termwise <integration> gives
$$
\boxed{\arctan x=x-\frac{x^3}{3}+O(x^5).}
$$
The integration constant is zero because $\arctan0=0$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The expression in the PDF is $1/a^x-1$, with the subtraction outside the fraction. Using the <exponential series> with $a^{-x}=e^{-x\ln a}$ gives
$$
\boxed{a^{-x}-1=-(\ln a)x+\frac{(\ln a)^2}{2}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$
For $a>0$, $a\ne1$, these are the first two nonzero terms. If $a=1$, the <function> is identically zero, so there are no nonvanishing terms.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The square is on the <cosine>: near zero, $\ln(\cos^2x)=2\ln(\cos x)$. Substituting $\cos x=1-x^2/2+x^4/24+O(x^6)$ into the <logarithmic series> gives
$$
\ln(\cos x)=-\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{x^4}{24}-\frac12\frac{x^4}{4}+O(x^6)=-\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{x^4}{12}+O(x^6).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ln(\cos^2x)=-x^2-\frac{x^4}{6}+O(x^6).}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $g(0)=0$, only the first three positive powers of $g$ contribute through degree three. The <composition coefficients of power series> follow by using $g^2=g_1^2x^2+2g_1g_2x^3+O(x^4)$ and $g^3=g_1^3x^3+O(x^4)$ in $f_0+f_1g+f_2g^2+f_3g^3+\cdots$. Hence
$$
\boxed{f(g(x))=f_0+f_1g_1x+(f_1g_2+f_2g_1^2)x^2+(f_1g_3+2f_2g_1g_2+f_3g_1^3)x^3+O(x^4).}
$$
Here $f_m$ and $g_m$ are coefficients of the <power series>, rather than raw <derivatives>; their definitions already contain any factorial factors.

= 4
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <exponential series> proves <normalization of a probability distribution>:
$$
\sum_{n=0}^\infty e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^n}{n!}=e^{-\lambda}e^\lambda=1.
$$
Let $M$ and $F$ count the two labels. Conditional on $N=M+F=k+l$, independent labeling gives a <binomial distribution>, so the <joint probability mass function> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb P(M=k,F=l)&=\mathbb P(N=k+l)\binom{k+l}{k}p^k(1-p)^l\\
&=e^{-\lambda}\frac{(\lambda p)^k}{k!}\frac{[\lambda(1-p)]^l}{l!}\\
&=\left[e^{-\lambda p}\frac{(\lambda p)^k}{k!}\right]\left[e^{-\lambda(1-p)}\frac{[\lambda(1-p)]^l}{l!}\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
Each bracket is a normalized <Poisson distribution>. Summing over $l\geq0$ therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(M=k)=e^{-\lambda p}\frac{(\lambda p)^k}{k!},\qquad M\sim\operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda p).}
$$
The factorization also proves that $M$ and $F$ are <independent random variables>. This is <Poisson thinning>; the phrase about ignoring the other count refers to taking a <marginal distribution>, rather than fixing that count.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $D$, $C$, and $V_0$ denote the three disjoint categories, and $+$ or $-$ the test result. The <law of total probability> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(+)=0.1(0.95)+0.2(0.9)+0.7(0.05)=0.31.}
$$
To calculate the requested <conditional probability>, first combine the two categories with the virus. Their joint negative-test <probability> is $0.1(0.05)+0.2(0.1)=0.025$, whereas $\mathbb P(-)=1-0.31=0.69$. <Bayes' theorem> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(D\cup C\mid-)=\frac{0.025}{0.69}=\frac5{138}\approx0.03623.}
$$
These numbers use the specified population proportions and test <probabilities>; the negative result reduces the initial combined proportion $0.3$ to about $3.62\%$ within this model.

= 5
{parent=Section B}
