= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Writing the <differential one-form> as $M\,dx+N\,dy$, its coefficients satisfy $M_y=e^y\cos x=N_x$. It is an <exact differential form>, with potential $F=(1+e^y)\sin x$, because
$$
dF=(1+e^y)\cos x\,dx+e^y\sin x\,dy.
$$
The implicit solution is therefore
$$
\boxed{(1+e^y)\sin x=C.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the real domain $y>0$, $M=y(x^2+\ln y)$ and $N=x$ have $M_y=x^2+\ln y+1$ and $N_x=1$, so this <differential one-form> is not exact. An <integrating factor for a differential one-form> is $\mu=1/y$, which is nonzero on this domain. Multiplication gives
$$
(x^2+\ln y)\,dx+\frac{x}{y}\,dy=d\left(\frac{x^3}{3}+x\ln y\right).
$$
Hence the implicit solution is
$$
\boxed{\frac{x^3}{3}+x\ln y=C,\qquad y>0.}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the interval $x>-1$ containing the <initial condition>, division by $1+x$ gives a <linear ordinary differential equation> with <integrating factor> $(1+x)^{-3}$. Consequently
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\bigl[(1+x)^{-3}y\bigr]=1+x,\qquad (1+x)^{-3}y=\frac{(1+x)^2}{2}+C.
$$
The initial value gives $C=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y=(1+x)^3\left[1+\frac{(1+x)^2}{2}\right].}
$$
This <polynomial> also satisfies the original equation at $x=-1$ and gives an extension to all real $x$. The <initial value problem> is uniquely determined on $x>-1$; the vanishing leading coefficient at $-1$ does not ensure unique continuation to its left.

= 6
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Put $S=\theta_1+\theta_2$ and $D=\theta_1-\theta_2$. The <gradient> has components $3\sin S+\sin D$ and $3\sin S-\sin D$, so a <stationary point> requires $\sin S=\sin D=0$. In the closed square, $S\in[0,2\pi]$ and $D\in[-\pi,\pi]$, and the compatible possibilities are precisely the four corners and $(\pi/2,\pi/2)$.

The <Hessian matrix> is
$$
\begin{pmatrix}3\cos S+\cos D&3\cos S-\cos D\\3\cos S-\cos D&3\cos S+\cos D\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its <eigenvectors> $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ have <eigenvalues> $6\cos S$ and $2\cos D$. The <second-derivative test> gives the following classification:

|| Point || $U$ || Hessian eigenvalues || Classification
| $(0,0),(\pi,\pi)$ | $-4$ | $6,2$ | <Minima>
| $(\pi,0),(0,\pi)$ | $4$ | $-6,-2$ | <Maxima>
| $(\pi/2,\pi/2)$ | $2$ | $-6,2$ | <Saddle point of a scalar function>

The corner classifications also hold for the smooth extension beyond the square, and hence for the restricted region. The global bounds $-4\leq U\leq4$ show that the stated <minima> and <maxima> are global within it. The central point has opposite curvature along the two diagonal directions.

\Image[paper-1-dipole-energy-contours.png]
{title=Energy contours show two minima, two maxima and a central saddle}
{description=The $U=2$ contour crosses at the saddle. Filled markers locate all five points where the <gradient> vanishes; color and labeled <level curves> show how the energy changes between them.}

