= Solution
{parent=i}

**False.** Take the <diagonal matrix> $A=\operatorname{diag}(1,2)$ and its <eigenvectors> $\mathbf x=(1,0)^T$, $\mathbf y=(0,1)^T$. Their sum $(1,1)^T$ is mapped to $(1,2)^T$, which is not a <scalar multiple> of that sum. More generally, $A(\mathbf x+\mathbf y)=a\mathbf x+c\mathbf y$ need not have the form $\lambda(\mathbf x+\mathbf y)$ when $a\ne c$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** By <linearity>, $(A+B)\mathbf x=A\mathbf x+B\mathbf x=(a+b)\mathbf x$. Since $\mathbf x\ne0$, it is an <eigenvector> of $A+B$ with <eigenvalue>
$$
\boxed{a+b.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** Applying the <matrices> in order gives $AB\mathbf x=A(b\mathbf x)=ab\mathbf x$. The <eigenvalue> is
$$
\boxed{ab.}
$$
This conclusion only uses the shared <eigenvector>; it does not require $A$ and $B$ to commute on other <vectors>.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v$ for a nonzero <eigenvector>, then $A^2\mathbf v=\lambda^2\mathbf v=A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v$. Thus $\lambda(\lambda-1)=0$, so
$$
\boxed{\text{every eigenvalue is }0\text{ or }1.}
$$
Both values need not occur: the zero <matrix> has only $0$, and the <identity matrix> only $1$. More precisely, an <idempotent linear map> whose <rank of a linear map> is $r$ has $r$ copies of $1$ and $n-r$ copies of $0$. Indeed, its <image of a linear map> and <kernel of a linear map> form a <direct sum>, giving an <eigenbasis> without assuming an <orthogonal projection>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On an <eigenvector> of $A$ with <eigenvalue> $\lambda$, the <linear operator> $B=I-A$ has <eigenvalue> $1-\lambda$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{spec}(B)\subseteq\{0,1\}.}
$$
The multiplicities are exchanged: if the <matrix rank> of $A$ is $r$, then $B$ has $n-r$ copies of $1$ and $r$ copies of $0$. Again, a value with zero multiplicity is absent.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Computing the <characteristic polynomial> with the convention $\det(\lambda I-H)$ gives
$$
\det(\lambda I-H)=\lambda(\lambda+1)(\lambda-3).
$$
Thus the three <eigenvalues> are
$$
\boxed{-1,\quad0,\quad3.}
$$
They can also be checked directly on the respective <eigenvectors> $(1,0,-1)^T$, $(1,-1,1)^T$, and $(1,2,1)^T$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the lowest <eigenvalue>, $-1$, solving $(H+I)\mathbf v=0$ gives $v_2=0$ and $v_3=-v_1$. A unit <eigenvector> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v=\frac1{\sqrt2}(1,0,-1)^T.}
$$
Any nonzero <scalar multiple> is an equally valid <eigenvector>.

= 9
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x\in(n,n+1)$, strict decrease gives $f(x)<f(n)$, while for $x\in(n-1,n)$ it gives $f(x)>f(n)$. Integrating over these unit intervals proves
$$
S_n=\int_n^{n+1}f(x)\,dx<f(n)<\int_{n-1}^nf(x)\,dx=S_{n-1}.
$$
Summing the rectangle bounds gives
$$
\int_2^{M+1}f(x)\,dx<\sum_{n=2}^Mf(n)<f(2)+\int_2^Mf(x)\,dx.
$$
The <partial sums> and the <improper integral> are nondecreasing. These bounds show that one is bounded exactly when the other is bounded, proving the <integral test for convergence>. In the convergent case, sum the same interval inequalities for $n=N+1,N+2,\ldots$ to obtain
$$
\int_{N+1}^\infty f(x)\,dx<R_N:=\sum_{n=N+1}^\infty f(n)<\int_N^\infty f(x)\,dx.
$$
For $f(x)=1/(x\ln x)$, the <antiderivative> is $\ln(\ln x)$, which grows without bound, so the first <series> **diverges**. For $f(x)=1/[x(\ln x)^2]$, the <antiderivative> is $-1/\ln x$, whose tail <integral> is finite, so the second <series> **converges**, with
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\ln(N+1)}<R_N<\frac1{\ln N},\qquad N\geq2.}
$$
Both <functions> are positive and strictly decreasing on $[2,\infty)$, as required.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <ratio test> uses $L=\lim_{n\to\infty}|a_{n+1}/a_n|$, when it exists for eventually nonzero terms: $L<1$ gives <absolute convergence>, $L>1$ gives divergence, and $L=1$ is inconclusive.

For $a_n=n/(2^n-1)$,
$$
\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\frac{n+1}{n}\frac{2^n-1}{2^{n+1}-1}\longrightarrow\frac12.
$$
Thus the first <series> **converges absolutely**. For the alternating second <series>,
$$
\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|=2\left(\frac n{n+1}\right)^5\longrightarrow2,
$$
so it **diverges**. Alternating signs cannot rescue terms whose <absolute values> fail to tend to zero.

= 10
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $x>0$ write $f(x)=x^{-a}I(x)$, where $I(x)=\int_0^xe^{-t}t^{a-1}\,dt$. Because $a>0$, the lower endpoint is <integrable>. At each positive upper endpoint the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives $I'(x)=e^{-x}x^{a-1}$. The <product rule> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{f'(x)=\frac{e^{-x}}x-\frac{a}{x}f(x).}
$$
Differentiating once more and substituting this expression for $f'$ gives
$$
\boxed{f''(x)=-\frac{e^{-x}}x-\frac{(a+1)e^{-x}}{x^2}+\frac{a(a+1)}{x^2}f(x).}
$$
Substitution now cancels the $e^{-x}$ terms and the terms proportional to $f$, proving $xf''+(1+a+x)f'+af=0$.

The <change of variables> $t=xu$ identifies $f$ as the <scaled lower incomplete gamma integral> $\int_0^1e^{-xu}u^{a-1}du$. It has a smooth extension at zero, with $f(0)=1/a$, $f'(0)=-1/(a+1)$ and $f''(0)=1/(a+2)$. Thus the apparent singularities in the derivative formulas cancel there; those formulas with denominators $x$ were derived for $x>0$.

