= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The kernel has an <integrable singularity> at the moving endpoint $t=x$. Direct substitution into the ordinary <Leibniz integral rule> would produce the undefined boundary value $h(x)/\sqrt{x-x}$ and the nonintegrable differentiated kernel $-h(t)/[2(x-t)^{3/2}]$. Those separately divergent expressions cannot be treated as ordinary finite terms. The <derivative of an Abel integral> is obtained by rewriting the <integral> before differentiating.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Assume $h$ is continuously <differentiable> on the interval concerned. For fixed $x>0$, take $u=h(t)$ and $dv=(x-t)^{-1/2}dt$, so $v=-2\sqrt{x-t}$. <Integration by parts>, interpreted first with an endpoint cutoff and then by a <limit>, gives
$$
g(x)=\left[-2h(t)\sqrt{x-t}\right]_{t=0}^{t=x}+2\int_0^xh'(t)\sqrt{x-t}\,dt=2h(0)\sqrt x+2\int_0^xh'(t)\sqrt{x-t}\,dt.
$$
The upper endpoint term vanishes, and the lower endpoint supplies the explicit square-root term.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Differentiate the rewritten expression. Its new kernel vanishes at the moving upper endpoint, and its <partial derivative> is an <integrable> inverse square root. The <derivative of an Abel integral> is
$$
\boxed{g'(x)=\frac{h(0)}{\sqrt x}+\int_0^x\frac{h'(t)}{\sqrt{x-t}}\,dt,\qquad x>0.}
$$
For a rigorous route, rewrite $2\int_0^xh'(t)\sqrt{x-t}\,dt$ as $\int_0^x\int_0^s h'(t)/\sqrt{s-t}\,dt\,ds$ using <Fubini's theorem>, and apply the <fundamental theorem of calculus> to the continuous inner <integral>. The assumption $h(0)\ne0$ implies $g(x)=2h(0)\sqrt x+O(x^{3/2})$, so the extension $g(0)=0$ has no finite right <derivative> at zero.
